			<div class="content" id="post-content-4419968">
									

<p>&#8230;. hm. Ultimately, the structure of this is what I take issue with. For what they have to convey, the logs drag on for far too long, unreasonably long. Cutting them to the three best ones would resolve that problem. But more than that, this suffers from an almost terminal case of note-at-the-end syndrome. There's no real justifiable reason for Gillespie's note to be a part of the official document. There's no insight into his research or whatever the justification was &#8212; its there for no particular reason and just serves as the author speaking directly to the reader about the article, handing over its secrets and mystery and point. The article is just a vehicle for a emotional prose-bomb at the end. And don't get me wrong, the prose is good. The scenes described are vivid and nostalgic and they absolutely do hit. The prose of the letter at the end is also tight and emotional. But ultimately the flawed structure of the article inhibits me from enjoying your impressive grasp on nostalgia.</p>

							</div>

								
