			<div class="content" id="post-content-4420000">
									

<blockquote>
<p>handing over its secrets and mystery and point.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Hm.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I'm not sure you're <em>wrong</em> exactly, but &quot;mystery&quot; wasn't my aim at all. This is a meditation on identity and how we maintain our sense of self against the intensity of vicarious experience. (For whatever it's worth, you're not correct that the end piece is meant to be me talking directly to the reader; I don't personally agree with Gillespie's revelation.)</p>
<p>Re: the justification for the personal note's existence, well of course it's contrived, but I don't think it's totally outlandish. Gillespie's used the headphones far longer than any previous test subject; his demeanor and personal outlook are relevant data for understanding the breadth of potential cognitohazardous effect (even if the apparent effect is ultimately minor).</p>
<p>It's a fair point that there may be too many test logs; I tinkered with their style and number more than once. It is probably ultimately a &quot;you mileage may vary situation.&quot;</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Sorry it didn't work for you - thanks for reading and for the thoughtful response!</p>

