			<div class="content" id="post-content-4420035">
									

<p>My statement on mystery and point was more a generalization on these kinds of articles as a whole &#8212; I probably could've been a bit more specific. When I say talking to the reader, I mean more - its a conversation between the reader and a character. In this scenario, Gillespie. Gillespie monologuing to the reader about identity and sense of self and so on and so forth. You don't have to agree with it to talk to the reader about it. By writing Gillespie, you're placing yourself in his point of view. You might well be right about the justification for the note; its contrived but not absurd. Unfortunately, too contrived for me to believe it. That said, it's not really a big deal. Suspension of disbelief is never a huge thing for me and that was largely auxiliary to my problems with it. So long as it does its job, which it does, its fine, I think.</p>
<p>I appreciate the response; just figured I'd clarify some of my less coherent thoughts.</p>

